@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s

@title Parser

@s From One File to a Source Model

A `.lit` file alternates explanation with named pieces of code. The
introduction showed a tiny file that writes `Hello` to `hello.txt`. We can make
its structure visible by keeping the output line in a named piece:

```text
@title A Greeting
@s Greeting
This small program writes one line.

--- hello.txt
@{Greeting line}
---

The text comes from a separate named piece.

--- Greeting line
Hello
---
```

Parser reads these lines once. It does not follow `@{Greeting line}`, decide
that `hello.txt` is an output file, or write anything. It records the prose and
code occurrences in source order, together with the locations and settings
that later stages will need.

@s The Model Built from the Example

For one input file, Parser returns a @code{Program} containing one
@code{Chapter}. The chapter contains the semantic sections begun by `@s`, and
each section contains its prose and code @code{Block} occurrences:

```text
Program "A Greeting"
`-- Chapter "A Greeting"
    `-- Section 1 "Greeting"              opening line 2
        |-- Prose                         body lines 3-4
        |-- Code "hello.txt"              opening line 5
        |   `-- "@{Greeting line}"        body line 6
        |-- Prose                         body lines 8-10
        `-- Code "Greeting line"          opening line 11
            `-- "Hello"                   body line 12
```

A block's opening and each of its body lines keep their own source locations.
The blank lines around the prose remain part of those prose occurrences. The
closing delimiter after `Hello` starts a possible new prose occurrence, but
end of input discards it because it has no nonblank text.

This is a source model, not a picture of the final HTML. Weaver can later turn
one semantic section into several numbered presentation sections. Resolver can
later combine several code occurrences with the same name. Parser preserves
the authored pieces needed by both operations without performing either one.

@s Following the Lines Once

Parser needs only three situations:

- In the **prologue**, before the first `@s`, it records page-wide commands
  such as the title.
- In **prose**, it appends ordinary lines until `@s` starts another section or
  a named `---` line opens code.
- In **code**, it keeps every line literally until an exact `---` closes the
  occurrence.

The example moves through those situations in a short sequence:

1. Line 1 records the title while Parser is in the prologue.
2. Line 2 creates section 1 and enters prose; lines 3-4 join its first block.
3. Line 5 stores that prose block and opens `hello.txt`. Line 6 remains the
   literal text `@{Greeting line}`.
4. Line 7 stores the code block and resumes prose; lines 8-10 join that block.
5. Line 11 stores the prose and opens `Greeting line`. Line 12 is literal
   target text, and line 13 closes the code.
6. End of input closes the section and returns the completed chapter.

The state machine described later implements exactly these transitions.
Commands, modifiers, Markdown examples, and malformed input add rules around
this path, but they do not change its basic shape.

@s The Parser Module

The generated module contains the source model, the public parsing boundary,
and the private state machine that joins them.

